WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.469 --> 00:00:05.270
Angular momentum measures how much something
is rotating.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:10.980
For a very brief time, a few of physicists
thought that Spin was a type of angular momentum

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.750
that came from the electrons spinning on their
own axis.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:20.140
These days we are pretty sure that isn’t
the case, electrons aren’t actually spinning,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.730
BUT spin is still thought of as a type of
angular momentum.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:31.499
It’s referred to as intrinsic angular momentum-
angular momentum a particle just has, without

00:00:31.499 --> 00:00:33.590
physically rotating.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:39.110
The idea always struck me as odd, and the
two arguments I’d heard for it seemed weak.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:44.390
The first is that in Quantum mechanics, even
though angular momentum and spin don’t obey

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.960
all the same equations, but they do both obey
a particular one.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.120
Then people claim that this equation defines
angular momentum- hence spin is a type of

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.380
angular momentum.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.620
I never understood why this random equation
was so important.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.670
The other argument, is what we looked at last
time, the stern-gerlach experiment and similar.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:10.000
There I thought the argument was, charged
particles that have normal angular momentum

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.170
act like magnets.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.660
Particles with spin also act like magnets,
so let’s say that they have a type of angular

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.530
momentum too- an intrinsic type.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:25.710
I thought that was unnecessary -couldn’t
you just say spin is an intrinsic magnetism

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.710
instead?

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.810
Isn’t that a simpler explanation?

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:35.150
In the last video, I asked you to research
spin for me- which you did a brilliant job

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.150
of.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.799
I did the homework too, by reading a bunch
of things and I realised I was wrong about

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:42.560
both those arguments!

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.690
There is a very good reason that suggests
this equation defines angular momentum- I’ll

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.280
explain why in the next couple of videos.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.200
But today I’ll talk about why I was wrong
about the stern-gerlach experiment, the angular

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.180
momentum isn’t just a useless by -product,
it’s actually pretty crucial.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.229
And so, maybe spin is angular momentum!

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:08.509
The mysterious Stern-Gerlach apparatus is
actually just a giant magnet.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:13.390
But a regular magnet wouldn’t be enough,
because if a little magnet was put into this

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.840
field, it would be equally pulled up and down.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.500
Instead, the top bit is made pointy because
that makes the south pole stronger, so the

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.170
magnet is pulled upward more.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:30.160
If the magnet was pointing downward, the south
pole strongly repels the little magnet’s

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.230
south pole, so it goes downward.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.200
So you can start to see how this machine might
measure orientation.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.410
It works fine for these two orientations,
but here’s the problem that bothered me

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:42.730
for weeks.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.430
Imagine I put it in on its side.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.959
What we want the machine to do is to not deflect
this magnet either way.

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:54.890
That seems fine, the magnet is equally attracted
and repelled from both sides, but you can

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:58.840
see, these forces are going to twist the magnet.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.160
They keep twisting it... until it’s pointing
in the upward direction.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.750
Now the force on the magnet is up!

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:12.030
This happens to any magnet pointed at an angle,
it gets twisted to point up.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.611
So by this logic, pretty much every magnet
should go straight upward...the Stern-Gerlach

00:03:16.611 --> 00:03:20.260
machine isn’t supposed to do this... so
what’s going on?

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:27.120
Turns out, I was wrong, this machine doesn’t
work for little bar magnets for exactly this

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.850
reason- but it does work when you have a charged
particle moving in a circle.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:38.379
I was making a big mistake; I thought these
two things were essentially the same, because

00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:40.650
their magnetic fields are the same.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:45.761
But the particles going in loops have one
extra property I completely neglected: they

00:03:45.761 --> 00:03:50.100
have angular momentum that comes from their
rotation.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.640
We can represent angular momentum with an
arrow.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.400
The length of the arrow represents how big
the angular momentum is, so if we rotate the

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.350
object faster, the arrow is bigger.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:07.269
The arrow also has direction, and this represents
which direction the object is spinning: the

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:11.379
arrow always perpendicular to the plane the
object is rotating in.

00:04:11.379 --> 00:04:16.500
The key thing about angular momentum is, it
doesn’t like to change.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.220
So that means it’s hard to speed up or slow
down the object, and it’s hard to tilt it,

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.750
because either of those things change the
arrow.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.430
Going back to our situation, if we have a
rotating object and we put it in a magnetic

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.690
field, just like a bar magnet, the magnetic
field will try to twist it.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:39.570
But if it did get twisted, it’s angular
momentum would change, so unlike the bar magnet,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:45.160
it’s going to resist the twisting, and that
has a pretty unexpected consequence.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.050
Instead of twisting this way, its angular
momentum traces out a circle.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:53.050
This is called Larmor precession.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.729
If you want to see the maths behind why that
is, I’ll explain it now.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:01.800
Otherwise, skip the explanation, and I’ll
just tell you why any of this matters for

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:02.800
spin.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.160
For this explanation we need our classical
mechanics.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.690
I’ll do my best to explain this, but it
took me ages to understand this properly,

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.660
so don’t panic if this is confusing.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.320
We need to introduce one more technical word:
torque.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.020
A torque is something that twists an object.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.830
In our case here, it’s from the magnetic
field.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:27.650
Just like angular momentum, torque is a vector,
and its direction is perpendicular to the

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:29.860
direction of the twisting.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.520
The magnetic field is trying to twist the
angular momentum this way, so the torque is

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.290
in this direction.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:41.260
Our angular momentum is currently in this
direction, and we want to know which way it

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:48.150
will be pointing in a very short time, delta
t, so we use this equation from mechanics:

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:55.360
The new angular momentum equals the old one
plus the torque times delta t.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.050
Multiplying the torque by delta t just makes
it smaller, and then we add that to our old

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.170
angular momentum.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:05.880
Using vector addition, the result is this
vector.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.900
You can see the angular momentum has started
to turn around this circle.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.949
If you think about it, the magnetic field
is still trying to twist it, but now in that

00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:19.639
direction, so the new torque is this way.

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:24.610
If we apply the rule again, the result is
a vector even further along, and if we keep

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.480
doing it, we can see that the angular momentum
processes.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.039
This was so hard for me to wrap my head around
because this torque wants to make the angular

00:06:33.039 --> 00:06:38.729
momentum point up, but it doesn’t make it
point any more up than it was before instead

00:06:38.729 --> 00:06:42.750
it makes it spin in the other direction.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:47.699
So if you put this thing with angular momentum
into the stern-gerlach machine, it wouldn’t

00:06:47.699 --> 00:06:49.919
just go flip up like a bar magnet would.

00:06:49.919 --> 00:06:55.880
In other words, the Stern-Gerlach machine
only works if you have angular momentum.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:00.710
When you put a particle that has spin in the
Stern-Gerlach machine, it does work -so by

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:07.210
this analogy, they should have angular momentum-
being magnetic is not enough.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:14.729
So maybe it is fair to say spin is an intrinsic
angular momentum... then again, all of this

00:07:14.729 --> 00:07:17.830
is an analogy from classical physics.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:21.800
Does Larmor precession even happen in quantum
mechanics?

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.120
Actually it does, and is pretty awesome.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.560
Say an electron is doing this quantum mechanical
larmor precession.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:35.000
If it’s gone around once, you’d expect
it to be in the same state it was originally....

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.000
but it’s not.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:39.550
You have to let it go around twice before
it’s the same again.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:45.340
Before this video ends, I want to thank you
so much for doing research for me last time!

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:48.710
I learnt a lot, and hopefully you did too.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:52.810
People told me about ideas I’ve never heard
off, and recommended a whole bunch of books

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:58.669
too, and so since that video, I feel like
my knowledge of spin and quantum mechanics

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:01.639
has increased a lot in the past few weeks.

00:08:01.639 --> 00:08:06.340
I want to do these research homeworks more
often- but there’s one big problem- a lot

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:10.400
of the sources on this type of material are
really advanced and confusing.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.200
So it could have been difficult to read for
a lot of people.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.710
How can we make sure that everyone can participate
in this sort of homework if they want to?

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:23.310
And also, I’ve been thinking about other
types of home assignments- do you guys have

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:26.400
any ideas of what you’d like me to try?

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.419
Now for this video’s actual homework
I’m interested, did you guys find this video’s

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:32.990
argument convincing?

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.989
Should we call spin intrinsic angular momentum?

00:08:35.989 --> 00:08:41.120
Is it weird to even try and explain spin with
classical ideas and using classical analogies?

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.440
Is intrinsic angular momentum inherently meaningless
phrase anyway?

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:50.410
Every time I ask these sorts of questions
I get a really diverse set of opinions all

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:53.439
well argued so this should be fun.

00:08:53.439 --> 00:08:57.800
Second, I mentioned that you can only get
an electron back to its original state by

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.690
rotating it twice.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:03.939
Last time, some people commented that this
isn’t so weird- some classical things do

00:09:03.939 --> 00:09:04.939
this.

00:09:04.939 --> 00:09:06.259
What do you think of this?

00:09:06.259 --> 00:09:08.749
Do you know an example?

00:09:08.749 --> 00:09:13.509
Finally for the classical mechanics fans,
I explained why a rotating thing in a stern-gerlach

00:09:13.509 --> 00:09:16.749
machine doesn’t just flip and point up.

00:09:16.749 --> 00:09:22.149
But now explain why the force that it experiences
is proportional to how much it was pointing

00:09:22.149 --> 00:09:23.510
up to start.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:30.779
Next time I’m covering my favourite bit
of all of physics, so I’m really excited.

00:09:30.779 --> 00:09:31.339
See you soon

